# No pipeline dogfood on this kit

This rule applies when the **workspace git root is a cursor-spells kit checkout**
(has `agents/csp-engineer-reviewer.md` and `skills/engineer-review/`, and is the
repo being edited as the kit — not a consumer app that merely installed the kit).

## Forbidden on this kit repo

Do **not** use the product shipping pipeline to develop the kit:

- Do not run `/csp-start-task`, `/csp-start-task --fast`, or `/csp-start-issue-task`
- Do not ask **Pipeline route** (`full` / `fast` / `issue`) for kit changes
- Do not dispatch nested Task `csp-software-developer` or `csp-bug-fixer` as the
  delivery path for kit work
- Do not drive kit changes through `approve-plan` → `finish-plan` → `update-docs` →
  `create-pr` as if this repo were a consumer product

Reason: dogfooding the pipeline on itself recurses (gates, orientation, and
ship stops fight the work that changes those same mechanisms).

## Required instead

- Edit kit files in the parent chat (or ordinary feature-branch git work)
- Use normal commits / draft pull requests for kit changes
- Use Jira only when the human explicitly wants a ticket — kit work may be
  ticketless

## Still allowed

- Manual **dogfood checklists** under `docs/superpowers/dogfood/` (scripts that
  verify kit behavior — not `/csp-start-*`)
- `/csp-harness-status`, `scripts/harness-bench.sh`, contract tests
- Running the pipeline against a **separate consumer project** that installed
  this kit

## Consumer projects

This rule must **not** be copied into consumer apps by `install-to-project.sh`.
Consumers keep `code-via-coding-agents` and run `/csp-start-*` as designed.
